you see all these dark boxes
that show a really large drift,

471
00:34:29,210 --> 00:34:30,690
Gemma doesn't really care that much.

472
00:34:31,890 --> 00:34:37,350
Gemma, no matter what language you
use to calibrate, the test results,

473
00:34:37,750 --> 00:34:39,250
they all look pretty okay.

474
00:34:39,710 --> 00:34:44,570
And so this really leads to believe that
it's going to depend on your model, which

475
00:34:44,570 --> 00:34:45,890
is the most annoying answer to give,

476
00:34:45,890 --> 00:34:51,790
because it means that no matter what
information I give today, the answer is

477
00:34:51,790 --> 00:34:55,010
still, at the end of the day, eventually
going to be, well, it depends.

478
00:34:57,070 --> 00:35:02,950
But it does matter, especially on
Quinn and likely on a large number of

479
00:35:02,950 --> 00:35:03,550
other models.

480
00:35:03,770 --> 00:35:07,450
It's just good to keep in mind that
not all models are created equally.

481
00:35:10,130 --> 00:35:14,230
Now, another concept is
the chat template tokens.

482
00:35:14,230 --> 00:35:18,170
I won't dive too deep into what they are,
but basically they're, if you don't know,

483
00:35:18,430 --> 00:35:22,410
there are special tokens that the model
recognizes as being, you know, basically

484
00:35:22,410 --> 00:35:22,970
instructions.

485
00:35:23,410 --> 00:35:29,010
So you can see this I am start is an
instruction to the model is indicating that a

486
00:35:29,010 --> 00:35:34,410
message is starting from the user and
the model knows how to parse that.

487
00:35:34,490 --> 00:35:38,250
And it's been trained to recognize that
that's the start of a new message from a

488
00:35:38,250 --> 00:35:38,450
user.

489
00:35:38,450 --> 00:35:41,850
Now, if you don't parse
these out normally, properly,

490
00:35:42,870 --> 00:35:48,450
it will split that I am start token
up into the normal tokens for a left

491
00:35:48,450 --> 00:35:52,730
bracket straight line I
am underscore start, etc.

492
00:35:53,550 --> 00:35:59,510
Now, this does introduce some issues because
in the actual running of the model is

493
00:35:59,510 --> 00:36:01,310
going to be parsing those special tokens.

494
00:36:01,310 --> 00:36:06,250
And that means that during the calibration,
if you don't parse them, it never saw

495
00:36:06,250 --> 00:36:10,670
what it looked like to receive those and
we never really looked at which weights

496
00:36:10,670 --> 00:36:11,230
activate.

497
00:36:11,390 --> 00:36:16,910
So if there are some weights that
activate a ton on only special tokens,

498
00:36:17,290 --> 00:36:21,410
and we don't ever show those to the model,
and then we run the model with special

499
00:36:21,410 --> 00:36:23,610
tokens, we're going to be in trouble.

500
00:36:23,610 --> 00:36:28,090
And this also, you know, technically is
what applies to, if we never showed it

501
00:36:28,090 --> 00:36:32,730
Chinese characters, but we expected to run
on Chinese characters, we're going to run

502
00:36:32,730 --> 00:36:33,230
into issues.

503
00:36:33,450 --> 00:36:34,250
This is the same thing.

504
00:36:35,310 --> 00:36:37,670
Typically, this has not
been an issue in the past.

505
00:36:38,290 --> 00:36:44,290
You can see here quen 3.5 4b doesn't
really care whether you've passed

506
00:36:44,290 --> 00:36:48,850
special tokens or not whether you've
wrapped the text in chat template tokens.

507
00:36:49,530 --> 00:36:53,530
Gemma, on the other hand, is the
first time this really came up a lot.

508
00:36:54,730 --> 00:36:56,590
Because something is special about it.

509
00:36:56,930 --> 00:37:02,110
To this day, I'm still not entirely sure
what, but it cares an extraordinarily large

510
00:37:02,110 --> 00:37:06,230
amount about the chat template tokens.

511
00:37:06,530 --> 00:37:12,310
If you don't use chat template tokens
in perplexity in the I matrix, it's a

512
00:37:12,310 --> 00:37:16,530
huge value that says like I had no idea
that this next token was arriving.

513
00:37:17,230 --> 00:37:21,590
It really, really, really wants to
see those special tokens first.

514
00:37:22,070 --> 00:37:23,950
It's been aggressively trained on that.

515
00:37:24,230 --> 00:37:27,570
It has no idea what to expect if it
hasn't received a special token.

516
00:37:27,570 --> 00:37:32,310
So this is why we had to

517
00:37:32,310 --> 00:37:36,090
look into the parsing of special tokens.

518
00:37:36,210 --> 00:37:41,970
This is why I worked on v6 to make sure
I would be passing chat templated tokens

519
00:37:41,970 --> 00:37:46,530
and conversations to try to get a bit
of a better idea of what will actually

520
00:37:46,530 --> 00:37:49,810
activate typically in a
model as you're using it.

521
00:37:51,090 --> 00:37:53,130
There are some other special edge cases.

522
00:37:54,210 --> 00:37:59,630
A mixture of extra models, for example,
have experts that end up getting starved,

523
00:38:00,010 --> 00:38:00,670
so to speak.

524
00:38:01,270 --> 00:38:07,170
So the idea being if you pass a bunch
of text to an MOE model, there's a

525
00:38:07,170 --> 00:38:12,030
very real chance that some of the experts
on some layers will never activate.

526
00:38:12,270 --> 00:38:13,330
They'll never see a token.

527
00:38:13,330 --> 00:38:18,750
And this is an issue because that
means we have no idea how to calibrate

528
00:38:18,750 --> 00:38:20,350
that expert.

529
00:38:20,610 --> 00:38:25,810
And especially when we're going to a very
low bit rates, if that expert does end up

530
00:38:25,810 --> 00:38:31,510
later getting activated by
something, it will behave

531
00:38:31,510 --> 00:38:32,110
incorrectly.

532
00:38:32,110 --> 00:38:37,230
Because we will have quantized it with
a potentially huge error not taking into

533
00:38:37,230 --> 00:38:41,970
account which specific weights we should
have kept at a higher precision, well, not

534
00:38:41,970 --> 00:38:43,990
higher precision, more accurately stored.

535
00:38:44,990 --> 00:38:49,270
And so you can see this
is a chart of which

536
00:38:49,270 --> 00:38:53,630
datasets starve the experts the most.

537
00:38:54,030 --> 00:38:59,550
If you pass purely random tokens,
46% of the experts will never see

538
00:38:59,550 --> 00:39:00,770
a single token.

539
00:39:00,910 --> 00:39:01,770
They'll never activate.

540
00:39:02,550 --> 00:39:07,570
If you pass just Hindi, this is
specifically quite in 3.6, 35B, A3B, if

541
00:39:07,570 --> 00:39:13,570
you pass just Hindi, 17% of
them will not ever activate

542
00:39:13,570 --> 00:39:15,450
English 12%.

543
00:39:15,450 --> 00:39:20,130
If you mix all eight languages, you'll
only see 7% of them don't activate.

544
00:39:20,130 --> 00:39:24,310
If you add code, 3.8,
if you add chat, 3.2.

545
00:39:25,430 --> 00:39:31,290
Mindset, which is V6, which I've posted
about, that was another one of my focuses

546
00:39:31,290 --> 00:39:35,950
with my latest dataset was to try to get
all the experts to activate or as many as

547
00:39:35,950 --> 00:39:36,350
possible.

548
00:39:36,990 --> 00:39:42,310
And the result of that was that only half
a percent of all of the experts never saw

549
00:39:42,310 --> 00:39:45,330
a token using the
dataset that I use today.

550
00:39:45,330 --> 00:39:47,850
And this changes between models.

551
00:39:48,070 --> 00:39:53,330
This is not a guarantee that every MOE
model will only ever have that low of a

552
00:39:53,330 --> 00:39:54,350
expert starvation.

553
00:39:54,790 --> 00:39:58,110
Some of them will have a much more, some
of them have absolutely no starvation.

554
00:39:58,810 --> 00:40:02,390
I am not an expert enough
to know why this occurs.

555
00:40:03,950 --> 00:40:06,110
But in theory, it makes sense.

556
00:40:06,710 --> 00:40:09,210
They've been trained on a very wide array.

557
00:40:09,330 --> 00:40:15,150
There's so many experts that there's
probably some that only ever activate

558
00:40:15,150 --> 00:40:17,130
in very, very specific scenarios.

559
00:40:17,270 --> 00:40:18,430
Maybe they're long context.

560
00:40:18,630 --> 00:40:19,930
Maybe they're very short context.

561
00:40:20,130 --> 00:40:24,110
Maybe it's when you're asking about
how to get to the local pharmacy.

562
00:40:24,110 --> 00:40:27,910
You never really know what those
experts saw during training.

563
00:40:29,030 --> 00:40:32,370
And in order to get them calibrated, that's
kind of what you would need to know.

564
00:40:34,720 --> 00:40:39,820
So looking at Q4KM with iMatrix, you can
