or PTQ with round and nearest where each

704
00:49:15,820 --> 00:49:20,120
trained weight is rounded to the nearest
of several levels, specifically 16 levels

705
00:49:20,120 --> 00:49:20,800
for orbit.

706
00:49:21,100 --> 00:49:22,760
The second is calibration.

707
00:49:22,760 --> 00:49:27,940
So methods such as I matrix and Lama CPP,
which Colin spoke about, and other methods

708
00:49:27,940 --> 00:49:33,700
like GPTQ and AWQ run sample text through
the model and use activation statistics to

709
00:49:33,700 --> 00:49:35,700
decide where rounding error matters most.

710
00:49:36,440 --> 00:49:40,760
And the third is to train with quantization
in the loop itself, which covers

711
00:49:40,760 --> 00:49:46,380
quantization aware training abbreviated as
QAT and quantization aware distillation

712
00:49:46,380 --> 00:49:47,960
abbreviated as QAD.

713
00:49:47,960 --> 00:49:53,920
So the idea behind QAT and QAD
is to train the model under the

714
00:49:53,920 --> 00:49:58,680
same conditions it'll run in once it is
quantized and to match those conditions as

715
00:49:58,680 --> 00:49:59,600
closely as we can.

716
00:50:00,040 --> 00:50:05,860
And so the result with Lama CPP formats is
a regular GGF with the same format size

717
00:50:05,860 --> 00:50:11,260
and speed as the PTQ version and with
most of the lost accuracy recovered.

718
00:50:11,260 --> 00:50:12,200
So

719
00:50:13,840 --> 00:50:18,640
as I said in the beginning today, I'll focus
on Lama CPP and its Q40 format, but the

720
00:50:18,640 --> 00:50:22,300
same principles apply to
other formats and runtimes.

721
00:50:23,020 --> 00:50:27,580
And since the aim is to match the deployed
format, we'll start with what this format

722
00:50:27,580 --> 00:50:29,080
does at inference.

723
00:50:29,360 --> 00:50:35,180
So in our case, it's a Q40 GGF running in
Lama CPP on a CPU, and there are a few

724
00:50:35,180 --> 00:50:35,860
things to reproduce.

725
00:50:35,860 --> 00:50:41,780
So the first is that the linear
weight matrices are stored as Q40 and

726
00:50:41,780 --> 00:50:43,540
a block holds 32 weights.

727
00:50:43,980 --> 00:50:48,040
And the scale is set by the weight with
the largest magnitude and each weight is

728
00:50:48,040 --> 00:50:49,600
stored as a 4-bit integer.

729
00:50:49,940 --> 00:50:53,200
So on average, this comes to
about 4.5 bits per weight.

730
00:50:53,740 --> 00:50:59,320
The second thing is the tide embedding
is stored as Q6K, a 6-bit format

731
00:50:59,320 --> 00:51:03,240
because Lama Quantize keeps this
sensitive tensor at a higher precision.

732
00:51:03,240 --> 00:51:09,240
And the third is on a CPU, Lama CPP quantizes
the activations going into the linear

733
00:51:09,240 --> 00:51:14,480
layers to 8 bits, the Q80 format, so the
dot product runs in integer arithmetic.

734
00:51:14,960 --> 00:51:19,020
For another format or runtime, this list
would be different, but the overall

735
00:51:19,020 --> 00:51:20,380
approach would be the same.

736
00:51:23,180 --> 00:51:27,300
So with QAD, there are two
copies of the same model.

737
00:51:27,580 --> 00:51:31,920
The teacher is the released full precision
model and it stays frozen through

738
00:51:31,920 --> 00:51:32,200
training.

739
00:51:32,200 --> 00:51:37,200
The student starts from the same weights,
so on every forward pass, the student's

740
00:51:37,200 --> 00:51:42,480
linear weights are quantized to Q40 and
immediately de-quantized, which introduces

741
00:51:42,480 --> 00:51:44,280
quantization noise during training.

742
00:51:44,680 --> 00:51:46,400
This is called fake quantization.

743
00:51:47,060 --> 00:51:49,520
So both models read the same text.

744
00:51:49,760 --> 00:51:54,420
At every assistant token, we take the
teacher's probability distribution over the

745
00:51:54,420 --> 00:51:59,620
entire vocabulary and minimize the forward
KL divergence from the teacher to the

746
00:51:59,620 --> 00:51:59,860
student.

747
00:51:59,860 --> 00:52:04,020
And there's no cross entropy term on
the labels, which happens in traditional

748
00:52:04,020 --> 00:52:05,220
quantization-aware training.

749
00:52:06,400 --> 00:52:11,200
And since rounding has zero gradient
almost everywhere, we use what's called a

750
00:52:11,200 --> 00:52:12,280
straight-through estimator.

751
00:52:12,800 --> 00:52:16,860
So the forward pass uses the rounded weights
and the backward pass treats rounding

752
00:52:16,860 --> 00:52:21,400
as an identity operation, so the gradient
updates on the float weights underneath.

753
00:52:21,400 --> 00:52:27,020
And because the student starts as an exact
copy of the teacher, all of the training

754
00:52:27,020 --> 00:52:29,240
signal comes from quantization error.

755
00:52:29,900 --> 00:52:34,700
And so after training, we export the
float weights and run the standard Lama

756
00:52:34,700 --> 00:52:38,060
quantized step, which
gives us a regular Q40 TGF.

757
00:52:40,660 --> 00:52:44,180
So this slide lists what
the student would simulate.

758
00:52:44,600 --> 00:52:47,820
So each item mirrors something
that Lama CPP would do on a CPU.

759
00:52:47,820 --> 00:52:51,920
So as I mentioned, linear weights would
use Q40 with the same block size and scale

760
00:52:51,920 --> 00:52:53,440
as the reference C code.

761
00:52:53,780 --> 00:52:57,960
The scale is recomputed on every forward
pass from the float weights, so it matches

762
00:52:57,960 --> 00:53:00,180
what Lama quantized
computes at export time.

763
00:53:00,900 --> 00:53:05,720
The activations going into those layers
are quantized to Q80 as the CPU kernels do.

764
00:53:05,920 --> 00:53:10,200
And we also similarly fake quantize the
tied embeddings to Q6K, the type that it

765
00:53:10,200 --> 00:53:11,980
would get in the Q40 file.

766
00:53:12,600 --> 00:53:17,960
So the training loss is distillation to
the teacher only, and the learning rate is

767
00:53:17,960 --> 00:53:21,900
quite small between 1e to the
negative 6 and 1e to the negative 5.

768
00:53:22,300 --> 00:53:24,040
And we tune this for each model size.

769
00:53:24,780 --> 00:53:27,380
Also, the training runs
themselves are quite short.

770
00:53:28,080 --> 00:53:33,780
We train on maximum of 10 to 20
billion tokens, which is 0.04 to 0.12

771
00:53:33,780 --> 00:53:36,580
percent of the tokens these
models see in training.

772
00:53:36,580 --> 00:53:41,320
But in fact, most of the gain comes
early and in runs where we evaluated

773
00:53:41,320 --> 00:53:45,740
intermediate checkpoints, the ones at a
couple billion tokens scored about the same

774
00:53:45,740 --> 00:53:46,440
as the final one.

775
00:53:48,960 --> 00:53:54,500
So here are five LFM 2.5 models
from our 230M to our 24B.

776
00:53:55,040 --> 00:54:00,980
Each row compares two Q40 files of the same
model as a percentage of its BF16 score.

777
00:54:01,460 --> 00:54:05,540
And the score is an average over knowledge,
math, instruction, following and tool

778
00:54:05,540 --> 00:54:06,140
use benchmarks.

779
00:54:06,140 --> 00:54:12,000
So the gray dot represents post-training
Q40 without any training, and the

780
00:54:12,000 --> 00:54:15,020
purple dot represents a Q40
checkpoint trained with Q80.

781
00:54:15,660 --> 00:54:21,300
So on the four smaller models, post-training
Q40 keeps about 87 to 93 percent of the

782
00:54:21,300 --> 00:54:26,120
full precision score, and Q80 lifts this
to about 97 percent, which closes between

783
00:54:26,120 --> 00:54:27,780
half and three-quarters of that gap.

784
00:54:27,780 --> 00:54:30,900
You'll also notice that the
smallest models gain the most.

785
00:54:31,120 --> 00:54:36,840
The 24B model loses less to begin
with, and Q80 takes it from about 97

786
00:54:36,840 --> 00:54:38,400
to 99 percent for Q40.

787
00:54:39,320 --> 00:54:43,440
Yeah, so you'll notice the absolute gain
is smaller on the large model, since in

788
00:54:43,440 --> 00:54:47,920
general larger models would tolerate
quantization better, but the percent recovery

789
00:54:47,920 --> 00:54:50,300
that Q80 closes is about the same.

790
00:54:51,840 --> 00:54:57,760
So this table puts Q80, Q40, next to
the formats that people would usually

791
00:54:57,760 --> 00:54:58,320
train from.

792
00:54:58,520 --> 00:55:02,760
As an example, we use our 350M model on
a Raspberry Pi measured with Lama Bench.

793
00:55:03,260 --> 00:55:07,440
And so you'll see that Q40 has the fastest
format, but the lowest score, and the

794
00:55:07,440 --> 00:55:13,120
usual way to try to get quality back is
things like iMatrix, calibration, or to move

795
00:55:13,120 --> 00:55:16,660
to a higher precision
format like Q4KM or Q5KM.

796
00:55:16,660 --> 00:55:21,580
But these formats use different kernels,
and on this CPU they process the prompt at

797
00:55:21,580 --> 00:55:23,400
a little over half the speed of Q40.

798
00:55:24,120 --> 00:55:30,120
But because Q80 is a plain Q40 file, it
has the same speed and memory of the Q40

799
00:55:30,120 --> 00:55:35,560
row, and it scores above plain Q4KM and
about a third of a point below Q5KM.

800
00:55:35,560 --> 00:55:41,460
And across the model size that we
tested, QAD for Q40 stayed above

801
00:55:41,460 --> 00:55:47,080
Q4KM on quality and with it about a half
point of Q5KM in a file that is about 13 to

802
00:55:47,080 --> 00:55:48,000
18 percent smaller.

803
00:55:50,780 --> 00:55:56,540
So a natural question is where the
source of recovery is from QAD, and

804
00:55:56,540 --> 00:56:02,520
one reasonable guess is that QAD moves
the weights closer to the 4-bit grid so

805
00:56:02,520 --> 00:56:04,380
that the rounding operation loses less.

806
00:56:04,380 --> 00:56:09,640
And we measured the rounding error of each
weight matrix relative to the size of the

807
00:56:09,640 --> 00:56:09,900
weights.

808
00:56:10,280 --> 00:56:14,320
After QAD, it's actually four to seven
percent higher than for the original

809
00:56:14,320 --> 00:56:18,580
checkpoint, so the weights end up
slightly further from the Q40 grid.

810
00:56:19,360 --> 00:56:21,860
We then measured the
outputs of the models.

811
00:56:22,280 --> 00:56:27,840
So on a fixed set of prompts from across
our benchmarks, we compared the next token

812
00:56:27,840 --> 00:56:32,760
distributions of the Q40 model on the full
precision teacher using KL divergence.

813
00:56:32,760 --> 00:56:38,200
And after QAD, this divergence is
substantially lower up to 40 to 50 percent.

814
00:56:38,880 --> 00:56:44,380
We also compared each Q40 file, so
after quantization with its own

815
00:56:44,380 --> 00:56:48,960
float checkpoint, which essentially is
measuring how sensitive this checkpoint is to

816
00:56:48,960 --> 00:56:53,420
being rounded, and that divergence
falls by 20 to 29 percent.

817
00:56:53,820 --> 00:56:59,200
So the gain appears to come from weights
whose rounding errors have less impact on

818
00:56:59,200 --> 00:57:00,360
the output of the model.

819
00:57:01,990 --> 00:57:07,750
A common question is how this differs from
standard quantization aware training, and

820
00:57:07,750 --> 00:57:12,350
so we ran this comparison on our 350 million
parameter model with the same data and

821
00:57:12,350 --> 00:57:13,150
same training budget.

822
00:57:13,970 --> 00:57:19,190
And QAD uses the exact same fake quantization
as QAD, and it trains on the text

823
00:57:19,190 --> 00:57:21,130
labels with traditional
cross entropy loss.

824
00:57:21,710 --> 00:57:26,970
We also included as a baseline plain fine
tuning followed by post-training Q40

825
00:57:26,970 --> 00:57:30,630
just to measure the baseline
effects of additional training.

826
00:57:30,630 --> 00:57:36,470
So post-training Q40 with no
extra training scores 32.5,

827
00:57:36,810 --> 00:57:40,590
fine tuning, and then quantizing,
and QAD both score below this.

828
00:57:40,690 --> 00:57:46,070
So they're well below this starting point,
whereas QAD scores above this at 36.1.

829
00:57:46,850 --> 00:57:51,390
And so what we found is that the
KL rows can help to explain this.

830
00:57:51,390 --> 00:57:57,090
QAD and QAD are equally robust to
their own rounding, but they are

831
00:57:57,090 --> 00:58:02,750
differing in the distance to the original
model where the QAD KL is at 0.51, and QAD

832
00:58:02,750 --> 00:58:05,930
is at 1.05, which is
further away than plain Q40.

833
00:58:06,930 --> 00:58:12,590
So a likely explanation for this is
what each method is training towards.

834
00:58:12,590 --> 00:58:18,310
QAD uses cross entropy on the text
labels, which pulls the model towards that

835
00:58:18,310 --> 00:58:21,490
particular text and away from
what the original model does.

836
00:58:22,050 --> 00:58:26,030
QAD uses the teacher's full output
distribution as the target.

837
00:58:26,350 --> 00:58:30,070
So the student only has to learn to
compensate for quantization error and try to

838
00:58:30,070 --> 00:58:32,730
replicate the distribution
of the original model.

839
00:58:32,850 --> 00:58:36,590
So there's a little bit less risk of
overfitting to that particular training text,

840
00:58:36,610 --> 00:58:40,970
particularly after the model has gone
through complex post-training and RL stages.

841
00:58:41,830 --> 00:58:46,570
The caveat, of course, is that this is one
model size recipe, so not to claim that

842
00:58:46,570 --> 00:58:51,950
QAD always reduces quality, just that we
personally found that distillation is a

843
00:58:51,950 --> 00:58:53,150
much more robust objective.

844
00:58:55,870 --> 00:59:01,830
We also found that the improvement that
we get from QAD is actually tied to the

845
00:59:01,830 --> 00:59:04,310
quantization format that
we're simulating in training.

846
00:59:04,310 --> 00:59:09,950
So on the left, we trained a 1.2
billion parameter model against Q40 and

847
00:59:09,950 --> 00:59:14,610
exported this checkpoint to a different
but very similar format called LPVQ, which

848
00:59:14,610 --> 00:59:18,630
is another format with a

849
00:59:18,630 --> 00:59:21,830
more constrained scale.

850
00:59:22,810 --> 00:59:28,810
And when we exported at Q40, the QAD checkpoint
gains 2.2 points, but when we export

851
00:59:28,810 --> 00:59:31,030
it to LPVQ, it only gains 0.5.

852
00:59:31,830 --> 00:59:37,770
And on the right is a similar test that
we do with iMatrix for our 24 billion

853
00:59:37,770 --> 00:59:38,450
parameter model.

854
00:59:38,690 --> 00:59:41,490
So we measured the KL divergence
to the full precision model.

855
00:59:42,010 --> 00:59:47,630
And so when you take a regular PTQ Q40
checkpoint, iMatrix lowers the divergence,

856
00:59:47,870 --> 00:59:48,770
which we expect.

857
00:59:49,230 --> 00:59:52,410
But on the QAD checkpoint, we
actually found that it raises it.

858
00:59:52,410 --> 00:59:58,170
So with an iMatrix, LamaCTP would pick each
block scale with a different rule than

859
00:59:58,170 --> 01:00:00,670
though from the one that
the model was trained with.

860
01:00:01,130 --> 01:00:05,990
So we recommend using a QAD checkpoint
in the format it was trained for without

861
01:00:05,990 --> 01:00:10,990
further calibration, which follows from
the idea at the start of the talk that the

862
01:00:10,990 --> 01:00:15,350
gain generally seems to depend on training
matching the deployment that we use.

863
01:00:18,160 --> 01:00:21,880
We also applied the same recipe
for our newer LFM3 models.

864
01:00:22,220 --> 01:00:26,700
And these QAD files are the Q40
files in our public GDR repositories.

865
01:00:27,220 --> 01:00:32,820
We found for our LFM3 1.7B model,
PTQ Q40 is roughly four points below

866
01:00:32,820 --> 01:00:34,860
BF16 on the average of some benchmarks.

867
01:00:35,380 --> 01:00:40,220
QAD narrows this and is only 1.4 points
below, which closes roughly about two-thirds

868
01:00:40,220 --> 01:00:40,700
of the gap.

869
01:00:41,260 --> 01:00:46,420
And this score is above Q4KM and within
a half point of Q5KM, both of which are

870
01:00:46,420 --> 01:00:47,140
larger files.

871
01:00:47,700 --> 01:00:51,040
We also replicated this
recovery across modalities.

872
01:00:51,460 --> 01:00:57,100
So for our vision model, the recipe uses
a Q40 language model with a Q80 vision

873
01:00:57,100 --> 01:00:57,500
encoder.

874
01:00:57,820 --> 01:01:02,940
And we found that on the average of some
vision benchmarks, QAD is able to close

875
01:01:02,940 --> 01:01:05,600
roughly 83% of the gap
induced by quantization.

876
01:01:08,120 --> 01:01:13,820
So to generally summarize this first
section of the talk, there are generally

877
01:01:13,820 --> 01:01:17,100
four points for people who are
trying to run these models locally.

878
01:01:17,640 --> 01:01:21,300
The first is that the quant name generally
would describe the storage format and

879
01:01:21,300 --> 01:01:23,860
doesn't say much about how the
weights themselves are produced.

880
01:01:24,500 --> 01:01:27,580
So two Q40 files of the same
model can score differently.

881
01:01:27,920 --> 01:01:32,300
And a label like QAD and QAT can tell you
that the weights were trained for that

882
01:01:32,300 --> 01:01:32,680
format.

883
01:01:32,680 --> 01:01:38,460
The second point is that we found that a
QAD checkpoint works best in the format

884
01:01:38,460 --> 01:01:43,240
it was trained for without additional
modifications of following training.

885
01:01:43,920 --> 01:01:48,480
The third is that the improvement was
largest mostly for our small models because

886
01:01:48,480 --> 01:01:51,240
they seem to lose the most
under 4-bit quantization.

887
01:01:52,200 --> 01:01:57,080
And fourth is with Q40, we picked this
format because it was the fastest on CPUs we

888
01:01:57,080 --> 01:01:57,400
measured.

889
01:01:57,400 --> 01:02:02,360
And QAD recovers most of the quality
that used to be the cost of choosing it.

890
01:02:02,900 --> 01:02:08,780
And so in this example, we used Q40 and
LamaCPP, but this same approach applies to

891
01:02:08,780 --> 01:02:12,060
any format where training can simulate
what the deployed model computes.

892
01:02:12,860 --> 01:02:18,240
And this command on our slide describes
how you can serve one of our QAD files with

893
01:02:18,240 --> 01:02:18,920
LamaCPP.

894
01:02:19,900 --> 01:02:22,380
That is the end of my first part.

895
01:02:22,600 --> 01:02:26,480
We have a blog post and also a workshop
paper coming that just described the method

896
01:02:26,480 --> 01:02:27,640
and measurements in more details.

897
01:02:28,120 --> 01:02:30,960
The QAD files are in our
repositories on Huggingface.

898
01:02:31,700 --> 01:02:34,240
Maxime will now talk about
how we evaluate these models.

899
01:02:34,240 --> 01:02:35,380
Thanks

900
01:02:36,920 --> 01:02:37,420
Aditya.

901
01:02:37,640 --> 01:02:38,080
Hello everyone.

902
01:02:38,760 --> 01:02:44,600
So yeah, this is a short section about
evaluations because in this talk you see that

903
01:02:44,600 --> 01:02:49,880
we have KAL divergence which
was also talked about before.

904
01:02:50,380 --> 01:02:51,480
And this is a nice proxy.

905
01:02:52,020 --> 01:02:56,340
The point with KAL divergence is that it
can hide some particular problem that

906
01:02:56,340 --> 01:02:57,600
happens to some domains.

907
01:02:57,600 --> 01:03:03,520
For example, to use the model can have in
general or the mixed mixture be very close

908
01:03:03,520 --> 01:03:09,340
to the original full precision model,
but can actually collapse in some ways.

909
01:03:09,560 --> 01:03:13,380
For example, it might not be able
to properly format the tool course.

910
01:03:13,760 --> 01:03:18,840
And you can find other examples like
this with long context, agentic task, et

911
01:03:18,840 --> 01:03:19,720
cetera, et cetera.

912
01:03:19,720 --> 01:03:25,540
So the idea when you do this
kind of work with QAD, QAD, but

913
01:03:25,540 --> 01:03:30,060
pretty much any quantization work is
how are you going to evaluate it.

914
01:03:30,560 --> 01:03:36,260
And here the main trade-off
is that GGUFs are not super

915
01:03:36,260 --> 01:03:42,240
fast when you want to do evaluations
at this scale on like 10 or

916
01:03:42,240 --> 01:03:43,520
hundreds of thousands of samples.

917
01:03:43,520 --> 01:03:49,340
So we need to find a trade-off between
not having too many samples and having

918
01:03:49,340 --> 01:03:55,200
enough samples that you can
still get some signal in order

919
01:03:55,200 --> 01:03:58,180
to understand if the quantization
is successful or not.

920
01:03:58,780 --> 01:04:01,660
So here we have selected for
this work seven benchmarks.

921
01:04:02,280 --> 01:04:05,900
We have GPQ Diamond and MMLU Pro,
but reasoning and knowledge.

922
01:04:05,900 --> 01:04:08,860
We have three benchmarks
of instruction following.

923
01:04:09,260 --> 01:04:15,220
We have one to use benchmark and
