1007
01:09:46,660 --> 01:09:49,220
So first, I want to talk about four bits.

1008
01:09:49,880 --> 01:09:54,560
You know, luckily, previous talks
have gone over quantization a bit.

1009
01:09:55,480 --> 01:10:01,140
But you know, here I have a sort of
histogram of a dynamic range of values here

1010
01:10:01,140 --> 01:10:03,260
between plus and minus six.

1011
01:10:04,280 --> 01:10:07,820
And obviously, if we're in a higher format
like the F16, we can represent a much

1012
01:10:07,820 --> 01:10:10,460
more fine-grained representation
of all the numbers in between.

1013
01:10:10,460 --> 01:10:14,920
And as we go down to FP8 or FP4,

1014
01:10:15,460 --> 01:10:20,660
we only have less and less
values or here I'm using codes

1015
01:10:20,660 --> 01:10:24,660
that we can represent on the given range.

1016
01:10:25,380 --> 01:10:29,600
And what we have here down on the red dots
are the distinct values we can represent

1017
01:10:29,600 --> 01:10:35,540
within the range of plus or minus
six for the common FP4 format E2M1.

1018
01:10:36,620 --> 01:10:41,100
Which means we have one bit for the sign
bit, whether it's positive or negative six

1019
01:10:41,100 --> 01:10:42,960
or any other value.

1020
01:10:43,280 --> 01:10:46,600
Two bits for the exponent, which
gives us our sort of dynamic range.

1021
01:10:47,480 --> 01:10:51,860
You can see the sort of dots get further
spaced away as we get to larger values.

1022
01:10:52,200 --> 01:10:56,280
And then one bit for the mantissa, which is
sort of the precision once we're in that

1023
01:10:56,280 --> 01:10:58,160
rough, you know, exponent range.

1024
01:10:58,160 --> 01:11:00,140
And you can see how we decode one value.

1025
01:11:00,480 --> 01:11:05,820
We have a zero bit for positive,
two bits for the exponent,

1026
01:11:06,480 --> 01:11:11,300
and one bit for the sort of precision
mantissa, which gives us three.

1027
01:11:13,900 --> 01:11:19,020
So I'm going to dive into an example of a
specific format, MXF before, I'll explain

1028
01:11:19,020 --> 01:11:19,740
a bit more later.

1029
01:11:20,120 --> 01:11:24,080
But generally, because the range of these
formats and the number of values they can

1030
01:11:24,080 --> 01:11:26,900
represent are so small,
just like 15 usable values.

1031
01:11:28,920 --> 01:11:30,980
We need to have some sort of scale.

1032
01:11:31,960 --> 01:11:37,940
And the micro scaling here is mapped
to the fact that we have pretty small

1033
01:11:37,940 --> 01:11:38,960
scale granularity.

1034
01:11:39,100 --> 01:11:45,080
So for a block of 32 weights, we'll
have one scale scaling every block

1035
01:11:45,080 --> 01:11:45,940
of 32 weights.

1036
01:11:46,100 --> 01:11:47,560
And then it can be a
different unique scale.

1037
01:11:48,120 --> 01:11:49,720
This is the format for MXF before.

1038
01:11:50,180 --> 01:11:55,880
And so we get the maximum sort of absolute
value of the block of weights we have.

1039
01:11:55,880 --> 01:12:00,160
Here we calculate 0.0451.

1040
01:12:00,460 --> 01:12:03,280
We're obviously having weights
that are pretty close to zero.

1041
01:12:03,760 --> 01:12:08,560
So if we just represented negative six to
positive six, we would be wasting a ton of

1042
01:12:08,560 --> 01:12:12,280
precision with the values
being so close to zero.

1043
01:12:12,480 --> 01:12:13,780
So we get the max.

1044
01:12:13,980 --> 01:12:16,340
We use this to calculate the scale.

1045
01:12:16,560 --> 01:12:18,420
Then we take the given weight value.

1046
01:12:18,660 --> 01:12:20,400
Here is what we have selected in red.

1047
01:12:20,400 --> 01:12:26,240
We divide it by the scale in order to
scale all of the weights to within the

1048
01:12:26,240 --> 01:12:28,480
usable range of plus or minus six.

1049
01:12:29,300 --> 01:12:35,140
And then we quantize the value to whatever
one of these gray lines, which are the

1050
01:12:35,140 --> 01:12:39,160
available levels of FP4.

1051
01:12:40,720 --> 01:12:44,280
And then we were able to save that
value in our quantized format.

1052
01:12:44,280 --> 01:12:50,160
And you can see the dark tick is the
stored value that's quantized to the

1053
01:12:50,160 --> 01:12:50,580
gray line.

1054
01:12:50,760 --> 01:12:54,180
And the lighter bar is where
the original value goes to.

1055
01:12:54,340 --> 01:12:58,200
And basically this little difference
you see on each one of these is the

1056
01:12:58,200 --> 01:12:59,060
quantization error.

1057
01:12:59,220 --> 01:13:04,700
Is how inaccurate or how
much, how inaccurately we are

1058
01:13:04,700 --> 01:13:07,720
mapping that value to the
value we actually store.

1059
01:13:09,200 --> 01:13:14,540
So obviously we can improve this
precision by doing finer grain scales.

1060
01:13:16,160 --> 01:13:18,140
There are some formats that do this.

1061
01:13:19,040 --> 01:13:22,880
One example I want to give is when you
have a block of 32 scales and you have one

1062
01:13:22,880 --> 01:13:24,900
outlier scale, outlier value.

1063
01:13:25,980 --> 01:13:27,380
That's much, much larger.

1064
01:13:27,540 --> 01:13:28,940
We were talking about 0.04.

1065
01:13:30,300 --> 01:13:33,820
And this one scale, sorry,
one value is now 0.3.

1066
01:13:34,120 --> 01:13:35,280
So an order magnitude larger.

1067
01:13:35,980 --> 01:13:39,560
So making a scale that represents
the max all the way up there

1068
01:13:40,480 --> 01:13:44,440
means that we waste a ton
of range on just one value.

1069
01:13:44,560 --> 01:13:48,220
And you can see the gray lines available
are just two for these smaller values.

1070
01:13:48,480 --> 01:13:50,260
And the quantization error is quite large.

1071
01:13:50,580 --> 01:13:56,540
So if we move to a smaller block scale
of 16, for mfp4, which I'll talk about

1072
01:13:56,540 --> 01:13:56,820
later.

1073
01:13:57,260 --> 01:14:01,540
These values in the smaller block are able
to get a much finer grain scale, less

1074
01:14:01,540 --> 01:14:02,300
quantization error.

1075
01:14:02,300 --> 01:14:07,820
And we just sort of lose quality
and the fine granularity on the

1076
01:14:07,820 --> 01:14:10,020
comparatively smaller block of weights.

1077
01:14:11,420 --> 01:14:15,640
So just hopefully some visual
intuition of the value of this.

1078
01:14:16,240 --> 01:14:21,980
And now sort of the important micro scaling
formats of the day that modern hardware

1079
01:14:21,980 --> 01:14:22,640
cares about.

1080
01:14:23,100 --> 01:14:25,240
There's mxfp4, mxfp8.

1081
01:14:25,240 --> 01:14:31,240
These are OCP standard formats, which
is a sort of open standard from many

1082
01:14:31,240 --> 01:14:32,260
different accelerators.

1083
01:14:32,660 --> 01:14:35,820
Accelerator companies, NVIDIA, AMD, Intel,

1084
01:14:37,140 --> 01:14:41,720
that have all agreed on sort of
shared micro scaling formats.

1085
01:14:42,220 --> 01:14:47,060
And then NVIDIA, of course, made their own
with mvfp4, which is a quite clever and

1086
01:14:47,060 --> 01:14:48,460
high quality format,

1087
01:14:48,620 --> 01:14:50,540
which you've hopefully
encountered since then.

1088
01:14:51,220 --> 01:14:54,700
All of these are supported
on Blackwell GPUs by NVIDIA,

1089
01:14:55,740 --> 01:14:57,160
the latest GPUs by AMD.

1090
01:14:58,080 --> 01:15:02,440
Also like Quaway Ascend, at
least for the mx formats.

1091
01:15:04,420 --> 01:15:09,760
Yeah, and it should be pretty clear
on, you know, we have our 32 values

1092
01:15:09,760 --> 01:15:12,780
that we're storing for the mxfp formats

1093
01:15:12,780 --> 01:15:15,840
and sort of one exponent-only scale.

1094
01:15:15,840 --> 01:15:21,760
And then mvfp4 has a smaller
granularity of one block scale for

1095
01:15:21,760 --> 01:15:22,740
every 16 elements.

1096
01:15:23,200 --> 01:15:28,440
And it uses a slightly different scaling
format, not purely exponent, but with some

1097
01:15:28,440 --> 01:15:29,780
anticipates for precision.

1098
01:15:30,320 --> 01:15:35,280
And it keeps a global scale at a
higher precision, fp32, in order

1099
01:15:35,280 --> 01:15:40,240
to keep lower precision

1100
01:15:40,240 --> 01:15:46,020
or sort of a better
precision granularity on

1101
01:15:46,020 --> 01:15:51,800
their scales, while still handling the
dynamic range with the single tensor scale.

1102
01:15:54,700 --> 01:15:58,820
So now that we're talking about the micro
scaling formats, I want to talk about

1103
01:15:58,820 --> 01:16:01,880
weight-only quantization versus
activation quantization.

1104
01:16:02,400 --> 01:16:07,680
This is a really important thing that it's
important you understand the distinction

1105
01:16:07,680 --> 01:16:09,700
from, so I'm going to go a
little bit more into that.

1106
01:16:10,280 --> 01:16:14,760
But, you know, obviously, when you're talking
about the memory usage, the disk usage

1107
01:16:14,760 --> 01:16:17,300
of a checkpoint, that's
weight-only quantization.

1108
01:16:17,640 --> 01:16:20,020
That's, you know, how many bytes
you're downloading from Huggingface.

1109
01:16:20,980 --> 01:16:25,780
That's usually done, you know, offline
with good calibration data or QAT, like

1110
01:16:25,780 --> 01:16:26,820
covered in the earlier talks.

1111
01:16:27,700 --> 01:16:32,460
And then activation quantization is what we
have to do if we want to actually do the

1112
01:16:32,460 --> 01:16:35,480
math in the lower precision that we,

1113
01:16:39,680 --> 01:16:39,680
you know,

1114
01:16:39,680 --> 01:16:41,660
the latest hardware supporting that.

1115
01:16:42,400 --> 01:16:47,100
And this is something that's usually, you
know, scales and quantization statistics

1116
01:16:47,100 --> 01:16:49,500
that we have to do
during the forward pass,

1117
01:16:50,140 --> 01:16:55,460
you know, by the kernel or the inference
engine and constantly computing these new

1118
01:16:55,460 --> 01:16:57,480
scales, quantizing the activations down,

1119
01:16:57,520 --> 01:16:59,680
and then dispatching the math.

1120
01:16:59,920 --> 01:17:05,480
And generally the format we use is WXAY,
where we talk about X bit weights and Y bit

1121
01:17:05,480 --> 01:17:06,160
activations,

1122
01:17:06,360 --> 01:17:09,180
and, you know, we'll sort of
go through different formats.

1123
01:17:09,180 --> 01:17:15,120
So, you know, as an example of talking
about the three different ways that we would

1124
01:17:15,120 --> 01:17:16,160
run 4-bit weights,

1125
01:17:18,000 --> 01:17:20,500
W4A16, we can run these on any GPU.

1126
01:17:20,720 --> 01:17:25,600
We just need some sort of kernel that can
deal with, you know, efficient unpacking

1127
01:17:25,600 --> 01:17:30,420
of the 4-bit weights into BF16 weights

1128
01:17:30,420 --> 01:17:35,540
to then do BF16 math as the activations
are kept in, you know, 16-bit quotes.

1129
01:17:37,400 --> 01:17:42,620
The key thing that we need a kernel support
to do this is that we don't want to, you

1130
01:17:42,620 --> 01:17:44,860
know, fully decontize the
weights ahead of time.

1131
01:17:44,940 --> 01:17:45,640
It's also slow.

1132
01:17:45,940 --> 01:17:51,860
If we want to load, you know, the weights
from your GPU's memory into shared

1133
01:17:51,860 --> 01:17:55,080
memory or whatever scratch
space each register has

1134
01:17:56,260 --> 01:18:00,680
so that, you know, we still get sort of
the memory bandwidth benefit of reading

1135
01:18:00,680 --> 01:18:04,560
those weights with less
memory bandwidth required.

1136
01:18:04,560 --> 01:18:09,400
Of course, W4A8, doing it now
with FPH activations instead.

1137
01:18:10,020 --> 01:18:13,640
This is what we would like to do
on, like, Hopper or Ada GPUs.

1138
01:18:14,220 --> 01:18:20,220
So we need to convert the
FPH4, in this case, weights to

1139
01:18:20,220 --> 01:18:21,040
FPH8.

1140
01:18:21,600 --> 01:18:26,660
And then we need to quantize down the
activations from BF16 to FPH8 dynamically.

1141
01:18:27,260 --> 01:18:29,520
And then we can do the math
in the FPH tensor core.

1142
01:18:29,520 --> 01:18:35,520
And, you know, finally on, like,
Blackwell GPUs, DGX Spark, we maybe have 4

1143
01:18:35,520 --> 01:18:37,160
-bit weights already available.

1144
01:18:37,540 --> 01:18:39,280
We want to use those
4-bit weights directly.

1145
01:18:39,640 --> 01:18:43,960
We need to swizzle them to
prepare them for the tensor core.

1146
01:18:44,060 --> 01:18:45,080
We can do that ahead of time.

1147
01:18:45,620 --> 01:18:49,420
And for the activations, we need to
quantize those down dynamically to FPH4.

1148
01:18:49,660 --> 01:18:52,280
And then we can do the math
in the FPH4 tensor core.

1149
01:18:52,560 --> 01:18:56,580
And the reason why you want to get to these
lower level tensor cores is you usually

1150
01:18:56,580 --> 01:18:57,800
get more flops

1151
01:18:57,800 --> 01:19:00,460
or floating point operations
per second available.

1152
01:19:01,740 --> 01:19:06,660
And I sort of have a representative graph
here that might make this, again, a little

1153
01:19:06,660 --> 01:19:08,380
bit more visually clear.

1154
01:19:09,000 --> 01:19:12,240
So on the y-axis, we have time
per gem or matrix multiplication.

1155
01:19:12,840 --> 01:19:15,800
And on the x-axis, we have the
number of tokens in your batch.

1156
01:19:16,140 --> 01:19:20,380
So when you're in this smaller
region of, like, 1 to 32 tokens,

1157
01:19:20,760 --> 01:19:25,540
this is probably just, you know, decoding
your single request or a few concurrent

1158
01:19:25,540 --> 01:19:26,480
requests you have.

1159
01:19:26,480 --> 01:19:30,820
And then as we go into the thousands of
tokens, we're clearly in pre-fill context

1160
01:19:30,820 --> 01:19:33,620
generation time.

1161
01:19:34,300 --> 01:19:39,140
And the little gray line is sort
of BF16 weights, BF16 math.

1162
01:19:39,660 --> 01:19:42,420
The blue line is 4-bit weights, BF16 math.

1163
01:19:42,980 --> 01:19:48,800
You can see this, you know, gives us the
benefit of, you know, faster time per

1164
01:19:48,800 --> 01:19:49,080
gem

1165
01:19:49,080 --> 01:19:52,440
when we're in this sort of
memory-bound low batch region.

1166
01:19:52,440 --> 01:19:58,120
And then if we're able to do the
red line of 4-bit weights and, like,

1167
01:19:58,180 --> 01:20:00,360
FPH math or 4-bit math,

1168
01:20:01,200 --> 01:20:04,280
we're able to not only get the
benefit in the memory-bound region,

1169
01:20:04,380 --> 01:20:08,140
but we're able to also get a benefit in
the compute-bound pre-fill region as well.

1170
01:20:08,200 --> 01:20:12,380
So hopefully making your time to first
hook in faster or handle larger decode

1171
01:20:12,380 --> 01:20:14,460
batches in production inference.

1172
01:20:16,540 --> 01:20:21,460
So, you know, the reason, again, why we
care about these lower precision math

1173
01:20:21,460 --> 01:20:26,620
is each major GPU generation, essentially,
in order to get greater speed-ups in the

1174
01:20:26,620 --> 01:20:27,400
previous generation,

1175
01:20:27,740 --> 01:20:32,880
has generally introduced some more aggressive,
some lower precision, some narrower

1176
01:20:32,880 --> 01:20:36,120
-bit math unit to their tensor cores.

1177
01:20:36,760 --> 01:20:38,020
And you can see this clearly here.

1178
01:20:38,200 --> 01:20:43,740
Technically, in Turing and Ampere, we had
int8 tensor cores and even, like, 4 tensor

1179
01:20:43,740 --> 01:20:44,120
cores,

1180
01:20:44,400 --> 01:20:48,580
but they sort of faced accuracy issues
with the activations getting quantized at

1181
01:20:48,580 --> 01:20:48,840
low.

1182
01:20:48,840 --> 01:20:52,580
So pretty much since
Hopper introducing FPH,

1183
01:20:54,200 --> 01:20:58,000
you know, that really made the
format popular in the community.

1184
01:20:58,700 --> 01:21:02,080
And then with Blockwell
introducing FPH4, same thing.

1185
01:21:02,340 --> 01:21:04,180
It's slowly gotten more popular over time.

1186
01:21:04,580 --> 01:21:08,140
And then now with Vera Rubin
coming in, latest from NVIDIA,

1187
01:21:08,540 --> 01:21:13,300
there's a new LUTB format of using
3-bit code books, which is quite unique.

1188
01:21:13,460 --> 01:21:15,360
And I'll talk a little
bit more about later.

1189
01:21:16,980 --> 01:21:22,660
But first, you know, I want to talk about
Humming, which is a relatively new library

1190
01:21:22,660 --> 01:21:28,160
or a library we've just announced in the
BLM project that I and others, particularly

1191
01:21:28,160 --> 01:21:29,140
people from Ant Group,

1192
01:21:29,480 --> 01:21:35,100
have been developing to deal with all this
complexity of mixing all the different

1193
01:21:35,100 --> 01:21:35,740
weight formats

1194
01:21:36,360 --> 01:21:40,480
users want to run in BLM, along with
all the different activation formats

1195
01:21:40,480 --> 01:21:43,540
that we have available for the
different hardware we're on.

1196
01:21:43,540 --> 01:21:49,500
And of course, trying to generalize
and manage this complexity on a wide

1197
01:21:49,500 --> 01:21:50,960
variety of NVIDIA GPUs

1198
01:21:50,960 --> 01:21:56,460
that supports all the way from
Turing GPUs, so your T4s, SM75,

1199
01:21:57,000 --> 01:22:01,360
all the way up to Rubin, DGXSparks, all
the way up to like, you know, SM121.

1200
01:22:02,060 --> 01:22:07,620
And it does this with a JIT compiled approach,
working on very fast JIT compilation,

1201
01:22:07,620 --> 01:22:13,280
not using Cutlass, but using a
specialized CUDA kernel template

1202
01:22:13,280 --> 01:22:13,660
-based,

1203
01:22:13,780 --> 01:22:15,460
which I'll talk a little
bit more about later,

1204
01:22:16,140 --> 01:22:21,280
and ultimately generating a specialized
kernel for that specific shape batch size in

1205
01:22:21,280 --> 01:22:21,560
GPU

1206
01:22:21,560 --> 01:22:25,320
that we're on, and Precision,
of course, as well.

1207
01:22:25,840 --> 01:22:30,240
And this comes as a result of us
working for years on, you know,

1208
01:22:30,260 --> 01:22:33,180
this sort of mixture of a
general weight quantization

1209
01:22:33,180 --> 01:22:36,300
and trying to mix in different
activation quantization as well.

1210
01:22:36,300 --> 01:22:39,760
You know, originally there was
Marlin kernels from ISD Austria.

1211
01:22:40,280 --> 01:22:44,580
We've integrated that as
in VLM pretty early on

1212
01:22:44,580 --> 01:22:49,180
and then developed it much further to
support, you know, FB4, MXFB4 weights and

1213
01:22:49,180 --> 01:22:49,480
things.

1214
01:22:49,880 --> 01:22:55,000
And then humming is our latest iteration to
hopefully eventually move on from Marlin

1215
01:22:55,000 --> 01:22:57,540
and build a more flexible
base for the future.

1216
01:22:58,760 --> 01:23:03,280
And here is the core part
about humming that, you know,

1217
01:23:03,280 --> 01:23:08,760
we came up with working on Marlin is you
sort of have this, you know, CUDA-based

1218
01:23:08,760 --> 01:23:10,120
template main loop

1219
01:23:10,120 --> 01:23:15,480
where you have a scheduler that tells you
what next block of, you know, outputs to

1220
01:23:15,480 --> 01:23:15,880
calculate.

1221
01:23:16,340 --> 01:23:20,260
You have some general way of
loading the weights, you know,

1222
01:23:20,260 --> 01:23:21,900
the compressed weights in
whatever format they are.

1223
01:23:22,380 --> 01:23:27,440
And then you have these abstractions to
transform or de-quantize your weight matrix

1224
01:23:27,440 --> 01:23:30,900
that can be implemented for each
of the different weight formats

1225
01:23:30,900 --> 01:23:34,760
and then mapping that to whatever
final format it needs to be

1226
01:23:34,760 --> 01:23:39,180
which we have many different, you know,
tricks or fused implementations of.

1227
01:23:39,400 --> 01:23:43,880
And then we have some sort of method for
running the actual tensor core operation

1228
01:23:43,880 --> 01:23:49,200
which depends on whether we're, you know,
what instructions are available on a given

1229
01:23:49,200 --> 01:23:49,520
GPU.

1230
01:23:49,960 --> 01:23:55,520
You know, on Turing we just have MMA, on
Hopper we have WGMMA, on Blackwell we have

1231
01:23:55,520 --> 01:23:57,060
tensor core Gen 5

1232
01:23:57,060 --> 01:24:02,960
and as well we have different, you know,
precision levels available for those tensor

1233
01:24:02,960 --> 01:24:03,280
cores.

1234
01:24:04,520 --> 01:24:09,060
And so all of this sharing the same
main loop and pipelining and scheduling

1235
01:24:09,700 --> 01:24:14,480
and we're able to specialize a bit the
different pieces of it and plug and play

1236
01:24:14,480 --> 01:24:20,040
so that adding a new
weight format suddenly,

1237
01:24:20,380 --> 01:24:20,840
you know,

1238
01:24:20,840 --> 01:24:24,300
allows us to run that weight format
with many different activation formats

1239
01:24:24,300 --> 01:24:26,120
and of course different GPUs.

1240
01:24:27,280 --> 01:24:33,080
So, yeah, you know, to start us off on
benchmarks, you know, here's an example on

1241
01:24:33,080 --> 01:24:34,480
an RTX 5090

1242
01:24:35,160 --> 01:24:38,160
just sharing different levels
of weight only quantization.

1243
01:24:38,420 --> 01:24:44,340
So this is doing WNA16
where N is from one bit

1244
01:24:44,340 --> 01:24:45,400
all the way up to 8 bits

1245
01:24:45,400 --> 01:24:51,220
and comparing against kubloss which is the
standard for, you know, B-float 16 math.

1246
01:24:51,220 --> 01:24:56,520
And you can see while we're in the sort of
smaller M region where we're going to be

1247
01:24:56,520 --> 01:24:57,280
more memory bound

1248
01:24:57,860 --> 01:25:02,340
as we go to here, you know, FP8,
we're roughly two times faster.

1249
01:25:02,960 --> 01:25:06,480
As we go to FP7, you know, we're
a little bit faster than 2x

1250
01:25:06,480 --> 01:25:11,060
and basically all the way till we go
to, you know, int1, you know, single bit

1251
01:25:11,060 --> 01:25:12,360
weights

1252
01:25:12,360 --> 01:25:16,560
not saying anything about accuracy of
that but, you know, humming as a library

1253
01:25:16,560 --> 01:25:22,560
and kernel generator can get the
performance particularly in, you know, the

1254
01:25:22,560 --> 01:25:23,300
memory bound region

1255
01:25:23,300 --> 01:25:26,460
of going for more aggressive
forms of weight only quantization

1256
01:25:26,460 --> 01:25:30,000
and of course giving you the
performance to run that efficiently.

1257
01:25:30,420 --> 01:25:34,980
And then as we scale all the way to the
very, very high ends of being compute bound

1258
01:25:34,980 --> 01:25:40,760
it doesn't, you know, regress performance
that much versus, you know, a BF16

1259
01:25:40,760 --> 01:25:42,840
native gem

1260
01:25:42,840 --> 01:25:47,380
which is good, you know, we're essentially
hiding the extra compute we're having to

1261
01:25:47,380 --> 01:25:47,640
spend

1262
01:25:47,640 --> 01:25:50,420
in doing the de-quantization and
then the matrix multiplication

1263
01:25:50,420 --> 01:25:54,840
through, you know, the efficient
pipelining of the main loop.

1264
01:25:56,740 --> 01:26:00,680
Now for the things that are actually, you
know, a bit more unique and interesting,

1265
01:26:01,520 --> 01:26:05,580
you know, one of the key things we've
developed in humming that's actually been

1266
01:26:05,580 --> 01:26:05,840
useful

1267
01:26:05,840 --> 01:26:09,820
for production inference
across thousands of GPUs

1268
01:26:09,820 --> 01:26:13,300
is running MXFB4 weights
on Hopper efficiently.

1269
01:26:14,440 --> 01:26:19,860
So, you know, generally, you know, DeepSeq
has released the deep gem kernels on

1270
01:26:19,860 --> 01:26:21,020
Hopper and Blockwell

1271
01:26:21,020 --> 01:26:26,440
for, you know, their production,
you know, FP8 deployment of,

1272
01:26:26,500 --> 01:26:28,620
you know, DeepSeq models

1273
01:26:28,620 --> 01:26:31,680
but also it's used for
Quen models or GLM models

1274
01:26:31,680 --> 01:26:34,120
or basically any model that's
released in FP8 these days

1275
01:26:34,640 --> 01:26:38,740
and they're really well optimized
for very high throughput and pre-fill

1276
01:26:39,580 --> 01:26:44,420
but they're less optimized for the sort
of low latency decode or memory bound

1277
01:26:44,420 --> 01:26:44,780
regions

1278
01:26:45,380 --> 01:26:48,940
and of course when we're just trying to
fit these larger and larger models of the

1279
01:26:48,940 --> 01:26:49,160
day

1280
01:26:49,160 --> 01:26:54,180
like Kimi K3 that's three trillion
parameters, it's really useful if we could

1281
01:26:54,180 --> 01:26:55,460
quantize the weights down,

1282
01:26:55,620 --> 01:27:01,120
keep them in, you know, FP4 rather
than needing to up-convert them to FP8

1283
01:27:01,120 --> 01:27:03,540
and thus require just more
GPUs to fit the model.

1284
01:27:03,540 --> 01:27:09,460
And so we're able to use Humming to,
you know, get similar or even much

1285
01:27:09,460 --> 01:27:10,840
better performance than DeepGem

1286
01:27:10,840 --> 01:27:16,680
for smaller M while decreasing the size

1287
01:27:16,680 --> 01:27:18,160
of the model in memory

1288
01:27:18,160 --> 01:27:24,100
and here even on this sort of existing
comparison of DeepSeq B4.1 Flash

1289
01:27:24,100 --> 01:27:27,760
where it's a model that's natively
released in MXFB4 weights

1290
01:27:30,060 --> 01:27:35,900
and MXFB8 activations were able to,
previously before Humming we were running it

1291
01:27:35,900 --> 01:27:39,980
with just the MXFB4 weights and
ignoring the activation quantization

1292
01:27:39,980 --> 01:27:44,680
with Humming now allowing the
activation quantization side as well

1293
01:27:44,680 --> 01:27:48,840
we're able to of course get a little bit of
better performance at small concurrences

1294
01:27:48,840 --> 01:27:49,640
with better tuning

1295
01:27:49,640 --> 01:27:53,500
but as we increase the concurrences,
get more compute throughput

1296
01:27:53,500 --> 01:27:57,060
we're able to see that benefit
of the activation quantization

1297
01:27:57,060 --> 01:28:01,760
and able to actually use those FP8 tensor
cores efficiently while serving this model

1298
01:28:01,760 --> 01:28:06,280
at the precision that it was intended
to be served, which is WW4AA.

1299
01:28:08,700 --> 01:28:12,980
Next on DJX Spark, similarly,
you know, I have GPT-OSS20B

1300
01:28:12,980 --> 01:28:16,600
which was also natively
released with MXFB4 weights

1301
01:28:17,620 --> 01:28:21,880
and originally we were using
Marlin to run it with WW4A16

1302
01:28:21,880 --> 01:28:27,300
but now if we use Humming with WW4AA
using the native FP8 support on

1303
01:28:27,300 --> 01:28:28,280
DJX Spark

1304
01:28:28,280 --> 01:28:31,220
we're able to see a little
bit of a benefit on decode

1305
01:28:32,280 --> 01:28:35,160
where we're able to already saturate
the memory bandwidth pretty well

1306
01:28:35,160 --> 01:28:38,800
but particularly on pre-fill where
we can really use the extra compute

1307
01:28:38,800 --> 01:28:42,980
we see up to 35% faster
time to first token

1308
01:28:42,980 --> 01:28:47,080
on DJX Spark which is cool.

1309
01:28:49,180 --> 01:28:51,440
So that's a little bit about Humming

1310
01:28:51,440 --> 01:28:53,060
and next I want to talk a
little bit about the future

1311
01:28:53,060 --> 01:28:55,000
and some of the things
we're working on now

1312
01:28:55,000 --> 01:28:59,860
which is thanks to NVIDIA
for early access on Verrubin

1313
01:28:59,860 --> 01:29:02,700
we've been looking into
the new LUT-B format

1314
01:29:02,700 --> 01:29:07,060
which LUT or LUT stands
up for lookup table

1315
01:29:07,060 --> 01:29:10,280
which is a pretty unique format

1316
01:29:10,280 --> 01:29:14,160
than what's commonly used
in quantization formats

1317
01:29:14,160 --> 01:29:19,060
we're not just quantizing the weights down
and storing those values in the format

1318
01:29:19,060 --> 01:29:23,520
we're now turning those
weights into 3-bit indices

1319
01:29:24,060 --> 01:29:26,340
into a lookup table or a codebook

1320
01:29:27,140 --> 01:29:32,320
which we can form that codebook
full of eight different

1321
01:29:32,320 --> 01:29:33,560
FP8 values

1322
01:29:33,960 --> 01:29:39,160
that maps uniquely to the distribution
of whatever we have in our

1323
01:29:39,160 --> 01:29:42,120
tile of weights

1324
01:29:42,120 --> 01:29:45,900
here the format uses an
8x64 tile of weights

1325
01:29:45,900 --> 01:29:50,420
so that's 512 weights that get
quantized down to 3-bit indices

1326
01:29:50,420 --> 01:29:56,400
we figure out what are the best 8 FP8

1327
01:29:56,400 --> 01:29:57,480
values to represent

1328
01:29:57,480 --> 01:30:01,160
within that range of 512 weights

1329
01:30:01,160 --> 01:30:04,100
and then we also have some
sort of scale exponent

1330
01:30:04,100 --> 01:30:10,000
that allows us to better scale things
down to represent within the FP8 codebook

1331
01:30:10,000 --> 01:30:15,280
and doing this altogether for
512 weights gets us down to 3.14

1332
01:30:15,280 --> 01:30:16,680
bits per weight

1333
01:30:16,680 --> 01:30:18,980
which is obviously less than FP4

1334
01:30:18,980 --> 01:30:23,080
and lets us save even more
memory bandwidth potentially

1335
01:30:23,080 --> 01:30:28,290
now one unique thing about this is it
actually doesn't work on the activations

1336
01:30:28,540 --> 01:30:30,320
at least in hardware support

1337
01:30:30,320 --> 01:30:33,020
the activations are actually done in MXFPA

1338
01:30:33,020 --> 01:30:35,740
so technically this is a W3A8 format

1339
01:30:35,740 --> 01:30:38,060
which has an interesting trade off

1340
01:30:38,060 --> 01:30:43,560
when you think about
running W4A4MVFP4 natively

1341
01:30:44,280 --> 01:30:47,680
so you are losing some compute intensity

1342
01:30:47,680 --> 01:30:49,680
in order for better memory bandwidth

1343
01:30:50,580 --> 01:30:52,960
however this can work quite well

1344
01:30:52,960 --> 01:30:56,540
since in production inference you
often have disaggregated inference

1345
01:30:56,540 --> 01:30:59,940
where you have some nodes
running pre-fill workers

1346
01:30:59,940 --> 01:31:01,760
some nodes working decode workers

1347
01:31:01,760 --> 01:31:05,700
so it would make sense that you
would run LUTB on your decode workers

1348
01:31:05,700 --> 01:31:06,940
where you are more memory bound

1349
01:31:06,940 --> 01:31:10,260
and you maybe run NVFP4
on your pre-fill workers

1350
01:31:10,260 --> 01:31:11,680
where you are going to
be more compute bound

1351
01:31:13,000 --> 01:31:17,360
so disaggregated quantization is
something we are already talking about

1352
01:31:17,360 --> 01:31:20,860
in the community and hoping
to introduce in the future

1353
01:31:21,560 --> 01:31:24,440
and just some quick things to share

1354
01:31:24,440 --> 01:31:27,600
on some experiments on GLM 4.7 flash

1355
01:31:27,600 --> 01:31:31,000
you can see there is the memory benefit

1356
01:31:31,000 --> 01:31:33,320
versus BF16 and NVFP4

1357
01:31:33,320 --> 01:31:39,020
we are able to store around 22% less

1358
01:31:39,020 --> 01:31:40,760
weights in memory

1359
01:31:40,760 --> 01:31:43,040
so we get a memory
compression benefit there

1360
01:31:43,040 --> 01:31:46,020
and from just simple online quantization

1361
01:31:46,020 --> 01:31:48,280
no calibration done at all

1362
01:31:48,280 --> 01:31:52,780
we are able to get decent
accuracy preservation

1363
01:31:52,780 --> 01:31:55,060
roughly on par with NVFP4

1364
01:31:55,060 --> 01:31:56,800
at least on a trivial GSMA-K

1365
01:31:56,800 --> 01:32:00,780
and we will be sharing a
lot more careful evaluation

1366
01:32:00,780 --> 01:32:06,020
and calibration of course
once we start making models

1367
01:32:06,020 --> 01:32:09,560
through this with GBTQ or AWQ

1368
01:32:09,560 --> 01:32:13,540
and iMatrix flows through Elm Compressor

1369
01:32:14,440 --> 01:32:16,940
on hugging faces we usually do at Red Hat

1370
01:32:18,600 --> 01:32:22,240
so what does this mean for
the GPU that you have today

1371
01:32:22,240 --> 01:32:24,520
and how it is going to run on PLM

1372
01:32:25,100 --> 01:32:27,620
if you have a model with 4-bit weights

1373
01:32:28,240 --> 01:32:30,640
we are going to run it as efficiently

1374
01:32:30,640 --> 01:32:33,560
and taking advantages as
much hardware as we can

1375
01:32:35,220 --> 01:32:38,300
on Turing, on Hopper, on Blackwell

1376
01:32:38,300 --> 01:32:40,060
and soon to be Ruben

1377
01:32:40,060 --> 01:32:43,700
and giving you the benefits of of course

1378
01:32:43,700 --> 01:32:45,800
the memory compression
which you value very much

1379
01:32:45,800 --> 01:32:47,380
to fit as many models as you can

1380
01:32:47,380 --> 01:32:49,240
on as little GPUs as you can

1381
01:32:49,240 --> 01:32:52,260
but also of course the
performance to run those models

1382
01:32:52,260 --> 01:32:55,140
at the most efficient
tokens per watt possible

1383
01:32:55,140 --> 01:32:58,120
and when you are using BLM

1384
01:32:58,120 --> 01:32:59,760
if you want to explicitly use Humming

1385
01:32:59,760 --> 01:33:01,420
you can specify the linear backend

1386
01:33:01,420 --> 01:33:03,900
for the dense gem or the Emily backend

1387
01:33:03,900 --> 01:33:06,560
but also you don't need to specify these

1388
01:33:06,560 --> 01:33:09,100
and BLM will choose the best
kernel backend by default

1389
01:33:09,100 --> 01:33:12,620
and Humming will hopefully
continue to become

1390
01:33:13,170 --> 01:33:15,260
the default more and more often

1391
01:33:16,220 --> 01:33:19,000
so yeah, that's all I have to share

1392
